\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



A bound $h:{\mathbb{N}}\to[0,\infty)$ is admissible if one signing $f$ works simultaneously for every starting point, positive length and common difference. The requested optimization is over admissible bounds; pointwise infima over different signings need not themselves be admissible.

A nontrivial $k$-term arithmetic progression is $a,a+d,\ldots,a+(k-1)d$ with $d\ne0$ and distinct terms, in the stated ambient group or space. In integer intervals the difference may be taken positive by reversing the progression. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/177}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Find the smallest $h(d)$ such that the following holds. There exists a function $f:\mathbb{N}\to\{-1,1\}$ such that, for every $d\geq 1$, $ \max_{P_d}\left\lvert \sum_{n\in P_d}f(n)\right\rvert\leq h(d), $ where $P_d$ ranges over all finite arithmetic progressions with common difference $d$. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
How slowly can a bound on signed sums along arithmetic progressions grow with their common difference, for one signing that works for all progressions?

